  "live_records": {
    "q-71830": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71830",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/f7a556f986c44b922908b79e3982e098eb7250d9d470092ba2c45bbffe190913.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=4.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/c55d5f809462b767122ccc4af97c5b4d243730ed24f655bdc18e35ec57e25ce2.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=\\frac{y}{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1.$ So, we have $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-71830-a",
