    "q-73549": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73549",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
